Denote by $a, b, c$ the length sides of triangle $ABC$. In triangle $ADM$ we have
$$
\begin{gathered}
DM^{2} = AM^{2} - AD^{2} = \frac{2\left(b^{2} + c^{2}\right) - a^{2}}{4} - \frac{4K^{2}}{a^{2}} \\
= \frac{2\left(b^{2} + c^{2}\right) - a^{2}}{4} - \frac{16K^{2}}{4a^{2}}
\end{gathered}
$$
$$
\begin{gathered}
= \frac{2\left(b^{2} + c^{2}\right) - a^{2}}{4} - \frac{1}{4a^{2}}\left(2 \sum a^{2}b^{2} - \sum a^{4}\right) \\
= \frac{1}{4a^{2}}\left(-2b^{2}c^{2} + b^{4} + c^{4}\right) = \left(\frac{b^{2} - c^{2}}{2a}\right)^{2}
\end{gathered}
$$
Since $\widehat{B} \geq 2\widehat{C} > \widehat{C}$, it follows $b > c$, hence
$$
\begin{equation*}
DM = \frac{b^{2} - c^{2}}{2a} \tag{1}
\end{equation*}
$$
The inequality $DM \geq \frac{AB}{2}$ is equivalent to $\frac{b^{2} - c^{2}}{2a} \geq \frac{c}{2}$, that is $b^{2} \geq c^{2} + ac$. Using the cosine law, the last inequality becomes $a \geq 2c \cos B + c$, or $\sin A \geq 2 \sin C \cos B + \sin C$. We can write $\sin(B + C) \geq 2 \sin C \cos B + \sin C$, hence $\sin(B - C) \geq \sin C$, and we get $2 \sin \frac{B - 2C}{2} \cos \frac{B}{2} \geq 0$. This inequality is true because $\widehat{B} \geq 2\widehat{C}$, and we are done.

Let $K$, $L$, $M$ and $N$ be the feet of perpendiculars dropped from point $E$ to lines $A B$, $B C$, $C D$ and $D A$, respectively.



Points $K$ and $N$ lie on the circle with diameter $A E$, hence $\widehat{E K N} \equiv \widehat{E A N}$. Similarly, $\widehat{E L M} \equiv \widehat{E C M} \equiv \widehat{E A N}$, hence
$$
\begin{equation*}
\widehat{E K N} \equiv \widehat{E L N} . \tag{1}
\end{equation*}
$$
In the same way we obtain
$$
\begin{equation*}
\widehat{E N K} \equiv \widehat{E M L} . \tag{2}
\end{equation*}
$$
From (1) and (2) it follows that $\triangle E K N \sim \triangle E L M$, therefore
$$
\frac{E K}{E N}=\frac{E L}{E M}, \text{ hence } E N=\frac{E K \cdot E M}{E L}=\frac{a c}{b} .
$$

